Subject: POLI: in Praise of Martial Arts Training

_________________________________________________________________
             FROM THE VIRTUAL DESK OF SANDY SANDFORT
    401 Merrydale Road * San Rafael, CA 94903 * 415 472 6525
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
I think almost everything Eric S. Raymond posted about taking a
"combat shooting" course and martial arts, was correct.  However,
I have three *little* nits to pick.  Eric wrote:

    ". . . developing what I think of as "warrior mind". . .
    Firearms training isn't so good for this, because even
    when the intent of firing a gun is defensive the effect
    is pure offense.  Also, and more importantly, the gun
    psychologically disconnects you from the violence.  You
    don't feel your bullet striking. . . . This is where
    martial arts comes in."

NIT PICK #1--Combat shooting IS a "martial art."  This is true in
the semantic sense, as well as in the philosophically sense (see,
Nit Pick #3).

NIT PICK #2--An *unfired* gun is no more "offensive" than a
clenched fist.  The idea that a gun must always be fired to be
effective is a myth promulgated by the gun grabbers.  Statistics
show that guns are *less* likely to be actually used (i.e.,
fired) than other types of weapons or techniques.  That means,
when you draw your gun, nobody says, "Okay Mr. Kung Fu, take your
best shot."  Demonstrations of force are rarely necessary when
you have a gun.

NIT PICK #3--I don't think shooters are any more "psychologically
disconnected" from violence than are Zen archers.  When I shoot,
I am VERY aware of my "action at a distance."  Oh yes, you DO
"feel" your bullet striking--and it feels good.

I'm all in favor of any type of self-defense training that works.
What I don't like is the smug air of superiority I get from
proponents of this or that flavor of martial arts.  (I DON'T
sense that attitude in Eric.  I think if he were to go through a
good combat firearms course, he would find the same mental and
emotional benefits he now gets from his unarmed martial arts.

If anyone is interested in taking a good combat firearms course,
I cannot recommend Massad Ayoob's Lethal Force Institute too
highly.  For more information, write Massad at LFI; P.O. Box 122;
Concord, NH 03301, or call, (603) 224-6814.

I Aim to Please,

