Date: Thu, 25 Feb 1993 19:44:43 -0500 (EST)
From: Carol Moore <cmoore@cap.gwu.edu>
Subject: SCI: Sunspot Cycles & Activist Strategy

     Many people in the 1980s predicted the freeing of the
Eastern Union and the dissolution of the Soviet Union.  But I
was probably one of the few who predicted (in writing) it
would happen in November/December of 1989--i.e. during the
height of the next sunspot cycle.
     Periods of high sunspot activity seem to correlated with
greater mass human activity and excitability, including
protests, riots, revolutions and wars.
    Sunspots are solar explosions which appear as dark
blotches on the surface of the sun.  During these solar
eruptions ultra violet radiation, electro-magnetic waves, and
electrically charged particles are spewed out into the solar
system, reaching the earth within a day.  This bombardment of
the earth causes magnetic storms, auraraborealis, fluctuations
in atmospheric pressure and electricity and in positive and
negative ionization, and increases the number of
thunderstorms, cyclones and earthquakes.  
    Humans are also sensitive to the effects of solar
eruptions.  Various studies link ultra-violet radiation and
changes in magnetic fields to increased metabolism levels or
erratic behavior.  And it is well known that while and
increase in negative ions makes us more energetic.  Increases
in admissions to mental hospitals and in traffic and
industrial accidents have been linked to the occurrence of
solar flares.       
     Considering that during a period of maximum sunspot
activity there may be as many as 200 solar flares in
one year, as compared with as few as 5 during a year of
minimum activity, it is not surprising that all these
atmospheric disruptions might similarly disrupt human
sensibilities.
    That sunspot cycle activity increased and decreased in a
cycle of approximately 11 years was established in the 1850s. 
During World War I, A.L. Tchijevsky, a Russian professor of
Astronomy and Biological Physics, noticed that particularly
severe battles regularly followed each solar flare during peak
period of 1917-18.  
     To test his hypothesis that the sunspot cycle
influenced human activity he constructed an Index of
Mass Human Excitability covering each year form 500 BC to 1922
AD.  He then investigated the histories of 72 countries during
that period, noting signs of human unrest such as wars,
revolutions, riots, expeditions and migrations, plus the
numbers of humans involved.  Tchijevsky found that fully 80%
of the most significant events occurred during the 5 years of
maximum sunspot activity.  (Tchijevsky's noting that the 1917
Russian Revolution occurred during the height of the sunspot
cycle earned him almost 30 years in Soviet prisons because his
theory challenged Marxist dialectics.)
     Sunspot activity does not directly cause humans
to rebel, riot, etc.  Rather it acts as an *irritant* to
trigger action by those already frustrated and angry about a
situation.  People who are seriously irritated will rebel,
riot etc. even at the low point of a cycle.  
     What the data indicate is that a greater number of people
engage in a greater number of riots, rebellions, etc. during
the 5 year period when there are the most sunspots, and
especially in the year period when there are the most
sunspots.  
  
     Longtime activists can probably remember periods of years
when people seemed lethargic and organizing was difficult and
others where activism flourished (like 67-69, 78-81, 88-91). 
If Tchijevsky is correct about humans greater excitability
during the sunspot cycle, then it is PRUDENT for activists to
be aware of these dynamics and not get discouraged during the
down part of the cycle when people are lethargic, like the
next 4-5 years, and start thinking about selling out or
"mainstreaming".  Nor should they fail to prepare for and take
advantage of the times of greater excitability--which will
start around 1997-98.  
  
     Some information about this can be obtained from The
Foundation for the Study of Cycles in Southern
California.  I haven't contacted them in a year or so but
their last phone number was 714)261- 7261.  3333 Michelson
Drive, Suite 210, Irvine, CA 92715.
  
     I have two pieces I could send if anyone is interested: 
Tchijevsky's article "Physical Factors of the Historical
Process" which I got about 8 years ago from the Foundation and
which you may or may not be able to get now. (I hope so!) 
Also, a chapter from CYLES: The Mysterious Forces that Trigger
Events by Edward R. Dewey with Og Mandino.  Plus an old,
longer article I wrote on the topic.
     
(I need 5.00 for postage/xerox/handling. Send cash or check
to:  Carol Moore, Box 65518, Washington, D.C. 20035.  )





