From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Subject: EXT: Kids vs. Uploading

> From: Nick Szabo <szabo@techbook.com>

>>  I want to live on
>>  personally, physically, in whatever mind and body is available to me.

> But are you living on "personally" and "physically" if you upload?
> Uploading involves destroying the entire body and just making a copy 
> of the memes; raising children involves merging genes and copying
> the memes in a rather error-prone but flexible manner.  

My first choice of method for living on would have to be via life extension
techniques and cell-repair nanotechnology. I have signed up for cryonic
suspension in case nanotechnology arrives too late. As for uploading, I
think that a few decades from now it would be too crude to allow an accurate
enough copy of the original mind, and so would not constitute a true survival
of the individual's identity. Hence such an upload would not be living on 
personally, just like if a parent dies and his / her children manage to survive
at least until, say, the heat death of the universe.   

In the long term, when uploading is advanced enough to be generally construed
as a genuine personal survival of the individual involved, I think it would be
stretching it to term this 'living on physically'. But by this time an
individual could choose to spend a while as an upload, then revert to a wide
selection of bodies depending on what tasks and lifestyle were to be carried
out over the period. This is morphological freedom. As long as the identity is
preserved, then the individual may live on personally, and would always have
the option of living on physically. I'd better explain my view of identity and
survival.

When a living, sentient, self-conscious being is reduced to invertebrates'
grub or burnt to ashes, it is clearly either a personal tragedy or the 
satisfying of a very entropian goal. Destruction is an experience which is to
one's grave disadvantage, as entropy has been vastly increased. But if
personal annihilation is a less extropian outcome than simply surviving and
remaining the same, then failing to boundlessly expand and transbiomorphose is
more entropic than choosing self-transformation. And in a very real sense, the
previous self ceases to exist after a massive transformation.  

At a given instant, each of us has a unique identity consisting of memories,
world-view, self-image, set of beliefs etc. A complete meme-complex. But I see
no problem with this identity changing as long as it fits into a subjective
continuum where changes are gradual and personal choice has some bearing on
them.

The following would be a permitted form of survival:-

:
:----------------->
:                     ------------> 
:                                  --------   ----------> 
:                                                        ------------->
:                                                                        
..........................................................................> t


The y-axis represents different identities, and plotting this against time
gives us the identity-line. In the above identity-line the horizontal gaps
denote periods of unconsciousness, biostasis or temporary lack of computing
cycles. These are perfectly allowable - if an identity ceases to be conscious
for six hours or six hundred years, it has still endured provided the change in
identity is within the maximum deemed permissible by the individual involved.

Below is a non-survival example:-

:
:------------>
:  
:
:
:
:            ------------------->
........................................> t


And this leads us on to Perry's 'matter of taste' and 'arational choice'. The 
scale of the vertical axes on these diagrams is subject to individual
interpretation. Some people would claim that they can 'live on through their
children'; I would say that parent and child are too distinct to be considered
the same individual. Even a clone or identical twin would not be the same
unless it had been born at exactly the same time and space, followed the same
world-line and incurred exactly the same experiences.

However, if you were to make an exact copy of me, with it standing a few feet
away from me as it was 'launched', I don't think I would regard myself as
having died if 'I' was shot, and the duplicate survived. If I was caught up in
a galactic catastrophe but my backups survived 2 million light years away, then
losing 2 million years' memories and downgrading to the individual I'd been
around 2 million years ago would be a bit of a blow, but it would not be death.
It would be starting the last 2 miliion years all over again (although this
time things might be better or worse, they could not be the same).

>>  It's a fact of life and death that all sentient, primitive, 
>>  genetically-evolved carbon-based lifeforms are programmed by their 
>>  DNA to 'want' to make themselves expendable by creating their 
>>  replacements

> Even with "want" in quotes that's stretching it.  We're programmed
> to want sex, which frequently led to having children until we
> learned how to short-circuit the process.  Demographics and examination 
> of our culture indicate there is very little desire for children
> themselves, compared to the desire for sex.   Also, having children
> does not make one "expendable"; people with children live just as
> long as those without (give or take some risk factors for having or 
> not having children, which are minor and roughly balance out in developed
> countries), and I can't imagine they would be any more prone to
> want to die.

But unfortunately most people still seem to want children (believing that it is
a form of 'immortality') more than they want physical, personal immortality
(or million-year lives). At a time when we can anticipate the success of 
nanotechnology, life extension advances, cryonics, uploading etc, of all the 
millions of people with a computer and modem only a couple hundred or so have
even elected to join the extropians list. Again it boils down to a matter of
taste. It is not for me to tell people what is good for them. But to me it
appears that the genes are still doing very nicely out of the whole
arrangement, whilst their creations get ripped off, tending to perish within
a few decades just as they are supposed to.  

Anyone looking at it objectively I think would have to agree that an individual
striving to survive (as per my definition) is acting more in his / her own best
interests than someone whose main ambition is to have children and then perish.
Under the altruistic deathist ideology, the next generation temporarily benefit
from the sacrifice of those who are making way for them, but then they have to 
be sacrificed in their turn ad infinitum. So no one wins -- every player loses. 
At the present time I think it would greatly benefit us Earthlings if a little
of the effort that goes into raising kids was diverted into establishing
genuine methods of survival, so that the opportunity for being a winner might
be open to everyone. 

Steve Whitrow                       sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk

