I said tend. My statement is therefore difficult to prove false, and you have
not proved that it is false. I think it is likely to remain a moot point.
> 
> BTW, I test for my red belt in Tae Kwon Do on Saturday.  Do you believe
> achieving it is going to make me suddenly harder to live with?
> -- 
No, but if we had duelling, and you were an asshole (neither of which appear to
be true) then it would. A dangerous asshole would be much more of a worry
than a weak one. What would you do if a man who has killed three people in
duels already told you that you should remove yourself because he hated your
offensive smell and personality? Take a good chance of dying for a point of
honour? Doesn't sound too smart. Or wait until he offends someone as dangerous
as him.
	I don't mind the custom per se - but any form of duelling that is not
non-lethal, and furthermore reasonably non-dependent on physical ability, is
likely to lead to a 'pplayground bully' mentality.
	A good example of duelling that I can agree with is the sonic stunner
duels practiced on Jinx, in the stories of Larry Niven. Perhaps tasers at
20 paces?
	(In N-Space, Larry describes being challenged to a duel by an anarchist
friend of his (over using his character in a story without permission - the
story 'Rule of Law' (I think), an anti-anarchism story) The duel is fought with
