From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu
Subject: ECON: Free-Market vets (conclusion)

   My cat (Midnight, because he's cold black, or he used to be before he
got gray hairs) was released from the animal hospital today. I found the
workers there to be kind and courteous.  The projected bill was $300, but he
recovered fast enough to be released a day early. The final bill -- to my
surprise and delight -- was $218.  The bill included his drug prescription,
a week's supply of his diet food, a complimentary cat carrier, and his
next 'checkup' a week from now. 

  I was very pleased with the service provided which was much better than
most human hospital treatment I've seen. The animal hospital even called
to check on his progress and to remind us to give him his medicine. The
service is probably so cheap and personal because this animal hospital is
a very small operation almost small enough to get the 'mom and opop' 
designation. I'm not complaining.

   What I've learned from all this, is that good cheap health care is possible
when the AMA, medicare/aid, and lawyers aren't involved. I know it isn't
very fair to compare human care to animal care, but when my cat's care
costs $50 a day, and my mother's care costed $2000 a day, something
is wrong  -- especially since I don't consider what was done to her 
that much better than being kept in a cage and watched by medical staff.




