Subject: SOC: Societies without murder...

   From: pgf@srl03.cacs.usl.edu (Phil G. Fraering)

   Perhaps these societies do have murder... it's just that in
   some societies, the tribe living over the hill isn't considered
   human, and to kill a nonhuman isn't murder...

Well, the one incident described in Chagnon's book that sticks in my
mind ran about as follows: A young boy was orphaned, and he was
"adopted" by his uncle.  However, the other children teased the boy,
and his uncle got tired of this, so he shot the boy with an arrow and
killed him.  Since noone objected, he was subjected to no sanctions.

However, what sticks in my mind is not so much that he "got away with
it", but that there was no indication that anyone looked down on him
for killing the boy.  That is, it appeared that in Yanamamo culture
killing someone is a pure tort -- noone who is not personally
inconvenienced by a killing feels any antipathy toward the killer.

This interpretation depends on the book being an accurate portrayal of
the culture, of course.

