Subject: LAW: custom vs first principles

   From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)

   Such mechanisms are ALWAYS dependant on someone objecting. If
   someone shoots you and no one cared to do anything about it, they'd
   get away with it no matter what some dusty books in the library
   said. Law is always dependant on custom -- you can't avoid that,
   period. Human law is not like physical law.

Yes, that's true.  But in a society with "police", there is someone
who is *obligated* to object if you are, say, killed.  Obviously, this
is imperfectly implemented, but it does raise the probability that
one's murder will be investigated.

   Our current society doesn't match it -- there is a notion of
   offenses against the state, and there is no competition in
   jurisprudence or enforcement services.

Well, yes, if your goal is to simply eliminate the police powers of
the state, that can be done.  The question I would like to see
answered is whether that can be done without dramatically increasing
the amount of crime that the individual is exposed to, or with a
dramatic increase in cost of policing.  That is, can one eliminate the
State police without making society "worse", in the ordinary sense of
the word.

